# LARAVEL CONTROLLER — COMPLETE LEARNING ROADMAP

This document covers the important controller-side concepts in Laravel, from beginner to advanced level.

The goal is to understand:
- How a request reaches a controller.
- How to receive and validate user input.
- How to interact with models and databases.
- How to send data to views and return different responses.
- How to handle authentication and authorization.
- How to manage transactions, exceptions, files, and relationships.
- How to structure controllers professionally.
- How controllers work with services, events, queues, and APIs.
- How to test controller functionality.

============================================================
1. UNDERSTANDING THE REQUEST LIFECYCLE
============================================================

When a user sends a request, Laravel processes it through several stages.

Typical flow:

Browser
    |
    v
routes/web.php
    |
    v
Middleware
    |
    v
Controller
    |
    v
Validation
    |
    v
Model / Service
    |
    v
Database
    |
    v
Controller
    |
    v
View / JSON / Redirect
    |
    v
Browser or API Client

Example:

Route::get('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'index']);

Controller:

public function index()
{
    $employees = Employee::all();

    return view('employees.index', compact('employees'));
}

Explanation:

1. The browser requests /employees.
2. Laravel matches the route.
3. Middleware runs.
4. Laravel calls the index() method.
5. The controller retrieves employees.
6. The controller sends the employees to a Blade view.
7. Laravel renders the view and returns the HTML response.

Important files:

routes/web.php
app/Http/Controllers/EmployeeController.php
app/Models/Employee.php
resources/views/employees/index.blade.php


============================================================
2. RECEIVING DATA FROM A REQUEST
============================================================

Laravel provides several methods for accessing request data.

Example request:

Name: Rahul
Email: rahul@example.com
Salary: 50000

Assume these values are submitted through a form.

2.1 input()

$name = $request->input('name');

Purpose:
Retrieve a particular input value.

You can provide a default value:

$name = $request->input('name', 'Unknown');

If the name input is missing, Laravel returns Unknown.

2.2 all()

$data = $request->all();

Purpose:
Retrieve all request input data.

Example result:

[
    'name' => 'Rahul',
    'email' => 'rahul@example.com',
    'salary' => '50000'
]

Important:
Do not pass all request data directly into database operations without validation and appropriate mass-assignment protection.

2.3 only()

$data = $request->only(['name', 'email']);

Purpose:
Retrieve only the specified input fields.

2.4 except()

$data = $request->except(['password']);

Purpose:
Retrieve all input data except the specified fields.

Important:
except() is not a replacement for validation. Explicitly select and validate the fields your application accepts.

2.5 query()

$search = $request->query('search');

Purpose:
Retrieve a query-string parameter.

Example URL:

/employees?search=Rahul

The search value is Rahul.

Use query() when you specifically want a value from the URL query string.

2.6 route()

$id = $request->route('id');

Purpose:
Retrieve a route parameter.

Example route:

Route::get('/employees/{id}', [EmployeeController::class, 'show']);

URL:

/employees/10

The route parameter id is 10.

2.7 has()

if ($request->has('name')) {
    // The input key exists.
}

Purpose:
Check whether an input key is present.

2.8 filled()

if ($request->filled('name')) {
    // The input has a non-empty value.
}

Purpose:
Check whether an input has a non-empty value.

2.9 boolean()

$isActive = $request->boolean('is_active');

Purpose:
Convert supported truthy request values into a boolean.

Useful for checkbox inputs.

2.10 file()

$file = $request->file('document');

Purpose:
Retrieve an uploaded file.

2.11 header()

$token = $request->header('Authorization');

Purpose:
Retrieve an HTTP header.

2.12 Request method

$method = $request->method();

Purpose:
Retrieve the HTTP method used for the request.

Examples:

GET
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE

2.13 Check the request method

if ($request->isMethod('post')) {
    // Handle POST-specific logic.
}

Important:
Request input methods retrieve data. They do not automatically validate whether the data is correct or safe.


============================================================
3. ROUTING AND CONTROLLER METHODS
============================================================

3.1 Define a route

use App\Http\Controllers\EmployeeController;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;

Route::get('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'index']);

3.2 Route with a parameter

Route::get('/employees/{id}', [EmployeeController::class, 'show']);

Controller:

public function show($id)
{
    $employee = Employee::findOrFail($id);

    return view('employees.show', compact('employee'));
}

3.3 Route-model binding

Route:

Route::get('/employees/{employee}', [EmployeeController::class, 'show']);

Controller:

public function show(Employee $employee)
{
    return view('employees.show', compact('employee'));
}

Laravel automatically attempts to find the employee using the route parameter.

If the employee cannot be found, Laravel returns a 404 response.

3.4 Named routes

Route::get('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'index'])
    ->name('employees.index');

Generate the URL:

route('employees.index');

Redirect to the route:

return redirect()->route('employees.index');

Benefits:
- Easier maintenance.
- Avoids hardcoding URLs throughout the application.
- Makes redirects more readable.

3.5 Resource controllers

Route::resource('employees', EmployeeController::class);

Laravel generates standard resource routes.

Common resource methods:

index()    - Display a list.
create()   - Display the creation form.
store()    - Save a new record.
show()     - Display one record.
edit()     - Display the editing form.
update()   - Update an existing record.
destroy()  - Delete a record.

3.6 Invokable controllers

An invokable controller has a single __invoke() method.

Example:

class DashboardController extends Controller
{
    public function __invoke()
    {
        return view('dashboard');
    }
}

Route:

Route::get('/dashboard', DashboardController::class);

Use this when a controller represents one specific action.

3.7 Middleware

Middleware runs before a request reaches the controller, or after the controller returns a response, depending on its implementation.

Example:

Route::get('/employees', [EmployeeController::class, 'index'])
    ->middleware('auth');

Purpose:
Only authenticated users can access this route.

Common middleware responsibilities:
- Authentication.
- Authorization checks.
- Request throttling.
- Request filtering.
- Other cross-cutting request checks.

============================================================
4. VALIDATION
============================================================

Validation checks whether submitted data meets your application's rules.

Never assume that data received from a form is valid.

4.1 Validate inside a controller

use Illuminate\Http\Request;

public function store(Request $request)
{
    $validated = $request->validate([
        'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'email' => ['required', 'email', 'unique:employees,email'],
        'salary' => ['required', 'numeric', 'min:0'],
    ]);

    $employee = Employee::create($validated);

    return redirect()
        ->route('employees.index')
        ->with('success', 'Employee created successfully.');
}

Explanation:

required:
The field must be provided and cannot be empty.

string:
The value must be a string.

max:255:
The value must not exceed 255 characters.

email:
The value must be a valid email address.

unique:employees,email:
The email must be unique in the employees table.

numeric:
The value must be numeric.

min:0:
The salary must be at least zero.

The validate() method returns validated data when validation succeeds.

For a traditional web request, failed validation normally redirects back with errors and old input.

For a JSON request, Laravel can return a JSON validation error response.

4.2 Use validated data

Correct:

$validated = $request->validate([
    'name' => ['required', 'string'],
]);

Employee::create($validated);

Avoid:

Employee::create($request->all());

Reason:
The request may contain unexpected or unwanted fields.

4.3 Update validation

When updating a record, exclude the current employee from the unique email check.

use Illuminate\Validation\Rule;

public function update(Request $request, Employee $employee)
{
    $validated = $request->validate([
        'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'email' => [
            'required',
            'email',
            Rule::unique('employees', 'email')->ignore($employee->id),
        ],
    ]);

    $employee->update($validated);

    return redirect()
        ->route('employees.index')
        ->with('success', 'Employee updated successfully.');
}

4.4 Form Request classes

For complex validation, create a dedicated Form Request.

Command:

php artisan make:request StoreEmployeeRequest

File:

app/Http/Requests/StoreEmployeeRequest.php

Example:

namespace App\Http\Requests;

use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\FormRequest;

class StoreEmployeeRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function authorize(): bool
    {
        return true;
    }

    public function rules(): array
    {
        return [
            'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
            'email' => ['required', 'email', 'unique:employees,email'],
            'salary' => ['required', 'numeric', 'min:0'],
        ];
    }
}

Controller:

use App\Http\Requests\StoreEmployeeRequest;

public function store(StoreEmployeeRequest $request)
{
    $employee = Employee::create($request->validated());

    return redirect()
        ->route('employees.index')
        ->with('success', 'Employee created successfully.');
}

Why use Form Requests?

- Keeps controllers cleaner.
- Groups validation rules in one place.
- Allows request authorization checks.
- Makes validation easier to maintain and test.

Important:
Do not leave authorize() returning true without considering whether authorization is required. Implement the appropriate authorization rule when necessary.


============================================================
5. AUTHENTICATION AND AUTHORIZATION
============================================================

Authentication:
Who is the user?

Authorization:
What is the user allowed to do?

Example:

Authentication:
Is the user logged in?

Authorization:
Is the logged-in user allowed to delete this employee?

5.1 Authentication middleware

Route:

Route::middleware('auth')->group(function () {
    Route::resource('employees', EmployeeController::class);
});

Purpose:
Protect the employee routes from unauthenticated access.

5.2 Authorization using a policy

Example:

$this->authorize('update', $employee);

Purpose:
Ask Laravel whether the current user is allowed to update this employee.

If authorization fails, Laravel normally returns a 403 response.

5.3 Authorization using a Gate

Example:

use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Gate;

if (Gate::denies('delete-employee')) {
    abort(403);
}

Purpose:
Check whether the current user has permission to perform an action.

5.4 Use authorization before sensitive operations

Example:

public function destroy(Employee $employee)
{
    $this->authorize('delete', $employee);

    $employee->delete();

    return redirect()
        ->route('employees.index')
        ->with('success', 'Employee deleted successfully.');
}

Important:
Hiding a Delete button in Blade is not sufficient authorization.

Users may send direct HTTP requests. Always enforce authorization on the server.


============================================================
6. CRUD OPERATIONS
============================================================

CRUD means:

Create
Read
Update
Delete

6.1 Create

$validated = $request->validate([
    'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
    'email' => ['required', 'email', 'unique:employees,email'],
]);

$employee = Employee::create($validated);

6.2 Read all records

$employees = Employee::all();

6.3 Read one record

$employee = Employee::findOrFail($id);

6.4 Update a record

$validated = $request->validate([
    'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
]);

$employee->update($validated);

6.5 Delete a record

$employee->delete();

6.6 Mass assignment

Laravel provides methods such as:

Employee::create($validated);

$employee->update($validated);

These methods assign multiple attributes at once.

The model must be configured to permit the attributes.

Example:

class Employee extends Model
{
    protected $fillable = [
        'name',
        'email',
        'salary',
    ];
}

Important:
- Only include fields that users are allowed to change.
- Never treat $fillable as a replacement for validation.
- Never assume a field is safe just because it appears in a form.

6.7 Soft deletes

A soft delete marks a record as deleted instead of immediately removing its database row.

Model:

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\SoftDeletes;

class Employee extends Model
{
    use SoftDeletes;
}

The table must contain a deleted_at column.

Delete:

$employee->delete();

Retrieve records including soft-deleted records:

$employees = Employee::withTrashed()->get();

Retrieve only soft-deleted records:

$employees = Employee::onlyTrashed()->get();

Restore a soft-deleted record:

$employee->restore();

Force delete:

$employee->forceDelete();

Use forceDelete() carefully because it permanently removes the record from the database.


