16. EVENTS, LISTENERS, NOTIFICATIONS, AND QUEUES
============================================================

These mechanisms help separate business operations from secondary actions.

Example:
When an employee is created, the application may need to send a welcome email.

Possible workflow:

Employee Controller
    |
    v
Employee Service
    |
    v
Create Employee
    |
    v
Dispatch Event
    |
    v
Listener
    |
    v
Send Welcome Notification

16.1 Events

An event represents something that happened.

Example:

EmployeeCreated

16.2 Listeners

A listener responds to an event.

Example:

SendWelcomeEmail

16.3 Notifications

Laravel notifications can deliver messages through supported channels such as mail and database notifications.

16.4 Queues

Queues allow suitable tasks to run asynchronously.

Examples:
- Sending emails.
- Processing large files.
- Generating reports.
- Performing slow external API calls.

Important:
Dispatching a queued job does not, by itself, guarantee that a worker is running.

A queue worker must be configured and running for queued work to be processed.

For business-critical operations, design the transaction and job-dispatch workflow carefully so that a job does not run before the relevant database changes are committed.


============================================================
17. API CONTROLLERS
============================================================

A web controller usually returns HTML views or redirects.

An API controller commonly returns JSON.

It is often useful to keep web and API controllers separate.

Example structure:

app/
    Http/
        Controllers/
            EmployeeController.php
            Api/
                V1/
                    EmployeeController.php

routes/
    web.php
    api.php

The API route file may require the API routing setup provided by your Laravel application.

17.1 Example API response

return response()->json([
    'data' => $employee,
], 200);

17.2 Create a JSON resource

Command:

php artisan make:resource EmployeeResource

Resource:

namespace App\Http\Resources;

use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Http\Resources\Json\JsonResource;

class EmployeeResource extends JsonResource
{
    public function toArray(Request $request): array
    {
        return [
            'id' => $this->id,
            'name' => $this->name,
            'email' => $this->email,
        ];
    }
}

Controller:

use App\Http\Resources\EmployeeResource;

public function show(Employee $employee)
{
    return new EmployeeResource($employee);
}

Benefits:
- Controls which fields are exposed.
- Helps keep API responses consistent.
- Separates the public response format from the database model.

17.3 API authentication

Laravel Sanctum can be used for supported API authentication scenarios.

Example route:

Route::middleware('auth:sanctum')->get(
    '/user',
    function (Request $request) {
        return $request->user();
    }
);

Choose the appropriate authentication approach for the type of client and authentication flow.

17.4 API versioning

Example URL:

/api/v1/employees

Versioning can help introduce future API changes without immediately breaking existing clients.

17.5 Rate limiting

Rate limiting helps control how frequently clients can make requests.

It is useful for protecting APIs against excessive requests and accidental overload.


============================================================
18. TESTING CONTROLLERS
============================================================

Controller behavior should be tested through HTTP requests where practical.

Laravel feature tests can test the request lifecycle, middleware, validation, database changes, and responses.

18.1 Example test

public function test_employee_index_page_is_accessible(): void
{
    $response = $this->get('/employees');

    $response->assertOk();
}

This test assumes that the route is accessible to an unauthenticated user.

If the route requires authentication, authenticate an appropriate user in the test.

18.2 Test validation

public function test_employee_requires_a_name(): void
{
    $response = $this->post('/employees', [
        'name' => '',
        'email' => 'rahul@example.com',
    ]);

    $response->assertSessionHasErrors('name');
}

This example assumes the POST route validates the name field and uses the traditional web validation response.

18.3 Test authorization

Test that users who are not permitted to perform an action cannot perform it through a direct request.

For example:
- An ordinary employee cannot approve another employee's leave.
- An HR user cannot perform an administrator-only action.
- A user cannot access another user's private document.

18.4 Test database changes

Use assertions such as:

$this->assertDatabaseHas('employees', [
    'email' => 'rahul@example.com',
]);

18.5 Important test categories

- Successful requests.
- Validation failures.
- Unauthenticated requests.
- Unauthorized requests.
- Missing records.
- Database changes.
- Transaction-related failures.
- File upload validation.
- API response structure.
- Concurrent or duplicate workflow operations where relevant.

Always make test assumptions match the actual routes, middleware, schema, and authorization rules in your application.


============================================================
19. OTHER IMPORTANT CONTROLLER-SIDE TOPICS
============================================================

Learn these concepts after mastering the fundamentals.

19.1 Dependency injection

Laravel can inject dependencies into controller constructors or action methods.

Example:

public function show(Employee $employee)
{
    return view('employees.show', compact('employee'));
}

Here, Laravel resolves the Employee route-model binding.

A service class can also be injected into a controller method.

19.2 Query scopes

Reusable database query conditions can be defined on models.

These help avoid repeating the same filtering logic.

19.3 Accessors and mutators

These control how model attributes are retrieved or assigned.

They are useful for attribute transformations, but should not replace validation or explicit business rules.

19.4 API pagination

Use paginated results instead of returning an unlimited number of records.

19.5 Caching

Caching can improve performance for appropriate data.

Consider:
- Cache keys.
- Expiration.
- Invalidation.
- Whether the data is user-specific.
- Whether stale data is acceptable.

19.6 Authorization policies

Policies organize authorization logic around model-related actions.

They are useful for applications with multiple user roles and ownership rules.

19.7 Idempotency

Idempotency means that repeating an operation does not incorrectly repeat its effects.

It is particularly important for payment processing, leave approval, balance deductions, and API operations that clients may retry.

19.8 Concurrency

Two users may submit conflicting operations at nearly the same time.

Learn:
- Database transactions.
- Row locking.
- Unique constraints.
- Optimistic concurrency strategies.
- Idempotent workflow design.

19.9 Audit logging

Audit logs help record who performed an important action, what changed, and when it happened.

Avoid recording passwords, tokens, or unnecessary sensitive information.

19.10 Performance monitoring

Learn to identify:
- N+1 queries.
- Slow database queries.
- Unnecessary model retrieval.
- Excessive external requests.
- Expensive synchronous tasks.


============================================================
20. WHAT SHOULD A PROFESSIONAL CONTROLLER LOOK LIKE?
============================================================

A controller should usually be easy to read.

Example:

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Http\Requests\StoreEmployeeRequest;
use App\Models\Employee;
use Illuminate\Http\RedirectResponse;
use Illuminate\View\View;

class EmployeeController extends Controller
{
    public function index(): View
    {
        $employees = Employee::query()
            ->with('department')
            ->paginate(10);

        return view('employees.index', compact('employees'));
    }

    public function store(
        StoreEmployeeRequest $request
    ): RedirectResponse {
        Employee::create($request->validated());

        return redirect()
            ->route('employees.index')
            ->with('success', 'Employee created successfully.');
    }

    public function show(Employee $employee): View
    {
        return view('employees.show', compact('employee'));
    }
}

This example assumes:
- The Employee model exists.
- The department relationship exists.
- The employees.index and employees.show views exist.
- The StoreEmployeeRequest class is configured correctly.
- The Employee model allows the validated attributes through its mass-assignment configuration.

A controller does not have to contain every possible method. Keep only the actions needed by your application.

============================================================
21. COMMON CONTROLLER MISTAKES
============================================================

Mistake 1:
Passing all request data directly to a model.

Problem:

Employee::create($request->all());

Better:

$validated = $request->validate([
    'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
    'email' => ['required', 'email'],
]);

Employee::create($validated);

Mistake 2:
Checking permissions only in Blade.

Problem:
A user can send a direct HTTP request.

Better:
Enforce authorization in the controller, policy, middleware, or another appropriate server-side layer.

Mistake 3:
Putting every business rule in a large controller.

Problem:
The controller becomes difficult to maintain and test.

Better:
Move reusable, multi-step business operations into service classes where appropriate.

Mistake 4:
Using all() on a large table.

Problem:
Too many records may be loaded into memory.

Better:
Use pagination, chunking, lazy processing, or another suitable retrieval method.

Mistake 5:
Ignoring N+1 queries.

Problem:
Repeated relationship access may generate excessive queries.

Better:
Use eager loading where appropriate.

Mistake 6:
Updating multiple related records without considering transactions.

Problem:
Partial updates can leave the database inconsistent.

Better:
Use transactions when related database operations must succeed or fail together.

Mistake 7:
Trusting user-supplied sort columns.

Problem:
Unrestricted sort parameters can produce errors or unsafe query construction.

Better:
Allowlist supported columns.

Mistake 8:
Catching exceptions and hiding the real problem.

Problem:
Unexpected failures become difficult to diagnose.

Better:
Handle expected failures meaningfully, report unexpected exceptions, and show safe messages to users.

Mistake 9:
Using the same controller for unrelated web and API responsibilities.

Problem:
Response formats and authentication behavior can become confusing.

Better:
Separate web and API controllers when the responsibilities differ.

Mistake 10:
Forgetting automated tests.

Problem:
Changes can break existing behavior without being noticed.

Better:
Test successful requests, validation, authorization, and important database effects.


